  "summary": "Current preprints and publications use tensor, deconvolution, and Gaussian process models to recover quantities absent from routine biological measurements.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is estimation of quantities that are not directly observed, drawn from longitudinal or multi-source biological data. A PARAFAC2 tensor decomposition of longitudinal microbiome data models subject-specific temporal shifts and delays, addressing temporal misalignment across subjects and outperforming CP. A systematic benchmark of cell-type deconvolution across cross-platform transcriptomic data and simulations evaluates how well these estimators recover cell composition, with log-normal regression (SpatialDecon) singled out. Deep Gaussian process imputation jointly models related ICU physiological streams and returns uncertainty-aware estimates for missing values. These studies differ in data type and learning framework, so the shared element is not a single method but explicit handling of uncertainty, temporal misalignment, or joint structure across multiple streams. The benchmark contributes a comparative reference rather than a new model.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
